        title: "The day the correct answer is the expensive one",
        body: "Publishing your own uncomfortable evaluation result ahead of a leak, three days before a launch, with an S-1 in drafting, is not a clever move. It is a costly one, and the cost is real and immediate: investor relations, a cold founder, a board member questioning your judgement. What it buys arrives later and compounds. It converts a leak into a disclosure, and the only variable that distinguishes those two words is who chose the timing. It creates a dated record of candour that regulators read for years and that materially narrows adversarial inquiries. And it tells your own safety organization that measuring uncomfortable things is safe, which is the sole reason anyone will measure the next one. Recognize this class of decision by its signature: the right answer produces a bad week and a good year, and every argument against it is about the week. You will face perhaps four of these in a decade and they are what the chair is for.",
