        title: "What the chair actually owns",
        body: "Gerald's closing question is the real curriculum. A frontier lab CISO does not own the security of the company in any operational sense — forty-one people cannot secure two thousand, and the mitigations that matter most live in other people's roadmaps. What the chair owns is three things. The process: that decisions of this class are made through a written mechanism rather than by whoever is most senior in the room, and that the mechanism has a way of being told it is broken. The record: that there exists a dated, consistent, honest account of what the company knew and when, because that record is what determines the outcome of every regulatory, legal, and press event for years afterward, and it can only be built prospectively. And the willingness: to be the most unpopular person in a room for twenty minutes, correctly, roughly four times a decade, and to be pleasant and useful for the other ten years so that the four times land. Notice how little of that is technical and how completely it depends on being technical enough to know which twenty minutes.",
